# A function invocation in an expression is one evaluation of the function
# (ed.3 7.3.2: an expression is evaluated by applying the operators to the
# values of its operands; 6.6.1.7: a function is executed once per
# invocation, its VAR_IN_OUT referring to the caller's variable). Each
# occurrence of BUMP(calls) therefore adds exactly 1 to calls and returns 2,
# so a cycle adds 2. calls is a VAR_OUTPUT of the program and keeps its value
# between cycles (initial value 0). Integer / truncates toward zero, MOD
# takes the sign of the dividend (table 30, as in ops/precedence).
#
# cycle 0: a=7   q = 7/2 = 3    m = 7 MOD 2 = 1     calls = 0+2 = 2
# cycle 1: a=-7  q = -7/2 = -3  m = -7 MOD 2 = -1   calls = 4
# cycle 2: a=10  q = 5          m = 0               calls = 6
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: a
outputs: q m calls
status: conformant
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 7.3.2, 6.6.1.7, table 30
---
0 a=7 => q=3 m=1 calls=2
1 a=-7 => q=-3 m=-1 calls=4
2 a=10 => q=5 m=0 calls=6
